<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valentinian II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">371–<a href="/years/392">392</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valentinian II (371–392) was western Roman emperor from 375, proclaimed at the age of four after the death of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a>. Dominated by his mother Justina, then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> and finally by the Frankish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a>, he never ruled independently. He was found hanged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> in 392, and Arbogast raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenius">Eugenius</a> to the throne in his place.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valentinian-ii.jpg" alt="Valentinian II" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brastite at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_emperor_Valentinian_II.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Valentinian was born to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> and his second wife Justina, and had three sisters: Galla, Grata and Justa. His half-brother Gratian had shared the imperial title with their father since 367. When the elder Valentinian died on campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> in 375, the leading generals and officials, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merobaudes-general">Merobaudes</a>, Petronius Probus, Equitius and Cerealis, did not acknowledge Gratian. They brought the four-year-old boy to Aquincum and named him augustus on 22 November 375. Historians suggest that the army and its Frankish general Merobaudes were concerned about Gratian's limited military skill and expected to command in the child's name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Gratian had to accommodate the generals who supported his half-brother. Valentinian was nominally ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, but Gratian in fact governed the whole West and Valentinian issued no laws. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> the boy grew up under the pro-Arian influence of Justina and the court, which brought conflict with the Nicene bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a>. In 384 Valentinian refused the petition of the pagan senators, led by Aurelius Symmachus, to restore the Altar of Victory. In 385 Ambrose declined an order to hand over the Portian Basilica for the court's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> service, and the people of Milan rallied to him.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a> killed Gratian in 383, Maximus crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in 386 to 387 and threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Valentinian and Justina fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, who married Valentinian's sister Galla and marched west in 388 to defeat Maximus. The restored emperor was installed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><p>Theodosius appointed his own supporters in the West, and modern scholars suspect he never meant Valentinian to rule, since he planned for his sons to succeed. When Theodosius returned east, the Frankish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a> became guardian of Valentinian and commander in the western provinces. A chronicler wrote that the emperor was shut up in the palace and reduced almost below the position of a private person.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Valentinian tried to dismiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a>, who publicly tore up the order. On 15 May 392 the emperor was found hanged in his residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>. Arbogast maintained it was suicide; many sources believe he had the young man murdered, ancient historians were divided, and some modern scholars lean toward suicide.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a>'s eulogy, the only contemporary Western source, is ambiguous. The body was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and laid in a porphyry sarcophagus beside Gratian, and Valentinian was deified. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a> then raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenius">Eugenius</a>, and Theodosius defeated both at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-frigidus">Battle of the Frigidus</a> in 394. Valentinian's reign foreshadowed the fifth century, when young emperors were controlled by powerful generals and officials.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valentinian II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emperors Theodosius I, Gratian and <b>Valentinian II</b> decreed that all subjects should follow the Christianity of the bishops of Rome and Alexandria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-27">27 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Augusta Treverorum, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Vienne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.76,6.64&amp;year=384" aria-label="Augusta Treverorum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Valentinian II"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5366 5853h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5366 5853h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.76,6.64&amp;year=384">What was here in 384 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
